## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cost-aware request routing, a practical constraint in real llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant budgeting and isolation
- Per-model pricing and token estimation
- Atomic budget updates
- Fallback routing and quotas

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for budget check and routing
async function routePrompt(tenant, tokens, model){
  const pricePerK = PRICE[tenant][model];
  const cost = Math.ceil(tokens/1000) * pricePerK;
  const ok = await Redis.decrbyfloat(`budget:${tenant}`, cost);
  if(ok >= 0){
    return forwardToModel(tenant, model);
  } else {
    // revert and fallback
    await Redis.incrbyfloat(`budget:${tenant}`, cost);
    return forwardToBackup(tenant);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for race conditions in budget updates across multiple workers?
- How would you handle dynamic price changes mid-session?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests designing a simple, resilient per-tenant model routing with clear failover, suitable for beginners while demonstrating practical data modeling, API surface, and basic testing.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant config: pinnedModelVersion, fallbackVersion
- Health checks and routing logic
- Failover handling and latency observability
- Simple durable storage for configs

## Code Example

```javascript
function chooseModelVersion(tenantId, healthMap, configStore) {
  const cfg = configStore.get(tenantId);
  const ok = healthMap[cfg?.pinnedModelVersion];
  if (cfg && ok) return cfg.pinnedModelVersion;
  return cfg?.fallbackVersion;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test race conditions between health checks and routing?
- How would you handle cross-region config drift?
